  "text": "Solution 3 is the most likely correct answer, though I could not verify it here and am relying on recall of the literature.\n\n- **Solution 1** gives $(n+7)/5$. Its sharpness claim rests on an unverified blow-up construction, and the $n/5$ scale looks too small for this problem.\n- **Solution 2** gives $(n+6)/5$. Its construction does avoid a rainbow $C_4$, but the upper-bound argument is only a sketch. The construction shows a lower bound on the threshold, not that $(n+6)/5$ suffices. Its claim that a $C_5$ blow-up is the unique way to avoid low color degree is unsupported.\n- **Solution 3** gives $(n+1)/3$. I recall a result stating that for triangle-free edge-colored graphs of order $n$ with $\\delta^c(G)\\ge (n+1)/3$, a rainbow $C_4$ exists for large $n$, and that the bound is sharp. The form $(n+a)/b$ with $b=3$ matches this recollection. I have not checked the sharpness construction, so this rests on memory.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
